course: dictionary
lesson: 5425
title: Words that carry expectations
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words tell you what people once expected of others. Ladylike is one of them. It describes behaviour that people used to think was polite, quiet and proper for a woman or girl, such as sitting neatly and speaking softly. «Her mum used to say be ladylike when Lily ran in the playground» shows that running about was not what some people wanted from a girl.
explain: Use the clues around the word. Running in a playground and being told to be ladylike go together, so the word is about how people expected a girl to behave. Ask: what would fit here? Then swap in your meaning, "be polite and quiet", and check that the sentence still makes sense.
explain: Old-fashioned grown-ups sometimes said «not ladylike» to stop girls running, shouting or getting dirty. This can be critical, which means it judges someone harshly. Good readers notice when a word is about what people expect, not about what is right.
fact: Ladylike describes behaviour people once thought was polite, quiet and proper for a woman or girl.
fact: «Not ladylike» was often used to stop girls running, shouting or getting dirty.
fact: Ladylike can be old-fashioned or critical, so read the sentence carefully to see how it is used.
fact: Modern lessons point out that these rules were unfair, and that good manners are for everyone.
fact: A good check is to swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense.
exercise: «She sat in a ladylike way, with her hands folded.» What does ladylike mean here? | Covered in mud | Very loud and fast | *Polite and neat, the way people once expected a girl to sit | Hungry
exercise: Lily's mum says «be ladylike». Why might Lily ask what it means? | Because she wants to stop reading | *Because the word tells her what people expected of girls, and she wants to understand the rule | Because she wants to run faster | Because it is a word for food
exercise: Modern lessons say the old rules were unfair. What do they say instead? | Running is always rude | Girls should stay quiet all day | *Good manners are for everyone | Only boys have good manners
test: «Lily asked what ladylike means.» What is the best meaning? | Loud and cheerful | Brave and fast at running | *Polite, quiet and proper, as people once expected of girls | Very dirty and muddy
test: Saying «not ladylike» was used to stop girls from doing what? | Sitting neatly | *Running, shouting or getting dirty | Speaking softly | Reading quietly
exam: Which best describes what modern lessons say about ladylike rules? | Running and shouting are always wrong for girls | Girls must always sit neatly and never run | Ladylike means loud and fast | *Good manners are for everyone, and the old rules were unfair
